Build a self-contained Python command-line downloader script that runs under
Termux on Android (shebang pointing to the Termux Python path) and is written
with `from __future__ import annotations`.
The script reads a list of URLs from a text file and downloads them concurrently
to a local directory, showing progress. **Dependencies and optional imports:** -
Required: `requests` (HTTP downloads) and `tqdm` (progress bars). - Optional:
`pycurl`.
Import it inside a try block and set a module-level flag such as `_HAS_PYCURL`
to True or False; when missing, set the module name to None and fall back to
other transports. - Optional: `loguru`.
If unavailable, provide a fallback logger shim object whose attribute access
returns a function that simply prints its message and arguments.
Expose the chosen object as `logger`. - Standard library only otherwise:
argparse, contextlib, hashlib, json, multiprocessing, re, signal, sys, time,
urllib (error, parse, request), and pathlib.Path.
Type hints use `typing.Optional` and PEP 585 style such as `list[str]`.
**Constants:** - Define a default desktop Chrome-on-Windows User-Agent string
(Chrome 120, AppleWebKit/537.36) as a module constant, used for HTTP requests
unless overridden. **Helper functions:** - `human_size(num)`: converts a byte
count (accepts any numeric or string-coercible value; returns the input as a
string if conversion fails) into a human-readable string with units B, KB, MB,
GB, TB, falling back to PB, using 1024 as the divisor and two decimal places
(e.g., "1.50 MB"). - `read_url_list(path: Path) -> list[str]`: reads a UTF-8
text file with errors ignored, splits into lines, strips whitespace, and returns
only non-empty lines.
Skip blank lines and handle read errors gracefully.
Lines beginning with `#` are typically treated as comments and should be
skipped, and duplicate URLs should be removed while preserving order. **Expected
behavior to implement (infer and complete the remaining logic consistently):** -
Use argparse with options for the input URL list file, output directory, number
of parallel workers (using `multiprocessing` pools or processes), user-agent
override (defaulting to the constant above), retry count and delay, timeout, and
optionally a flag to force pycurl or requests. - Derive a safe local filename
from each URL using `urllib.parse` (taking the last path segment, decoding
percent-encoding, falling back to a hash-based name via `hashlib` such as an MD5
or SHA-1 prefix when the name is empty or invalid).
Sanitize filenames with `re` to strip unsafe characters. - Stream downloads to
disk in chunks, using `requests` with a session and the chosen User-Agent,
optionally switching to `pycurl` when available and requested.
Show a per-file or overall `tqdm` progress bar, and report sizes via
`human_size`. - Support resume-or-skip: if the target file already exists with
matching size or a recorded state, skip it; write to a temporary `.part` file
and rename on completion. - Retry transient failures (connection errors,
timeouts, 5xx responses) with the configured delay; do not retry 4xx client
errors beyond reporting them.
Catch `urllib.error` and `requests` exceptions and log them through `logger`
without crashing the whole batch. - Handle `SIGINT` (Ctrl+C) gracefully via
`signal`: stop dispatching new work, terminate workers, clean up partial files,
and exit with a nonzero status. - Optionally persist a JSON summary (counts of
succeeded, skipped, and failed URLs with error messages and sizes) at the end of
the run, and print a final summary. - Use `time` to measure elapsed time and
average throughput for the final report. - Exit with status 0 if all downloads
succeeded or were skipped, and a nonzero status if any failed; print a usage
message on invalid arguments via argparse. **Entry point:** Guard the main logic
with `if __name__ == "__main__":` and call a `main()` function that returns an
integer exit code, passed to `sys.exit`. **Output requirements for the
reproduced script:** Produce a single complete Python file that follows the
structure above, with clear logging through the logger, idiomatic naming, and
comments matching the density of a typical small utility script. ---
